  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Collybia Clouds (Syzygospora mycetophila) is a small basidiomycete fungus belonging to the Filobasidiaceae family, characterized by its delicate, cloud-like fruiting structures. This gelatinous fungus is commonly observed in New England forests during moist conditions.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Gelatinous Texture",
      "description": "The fruiting bodies have a distinctive jelly-like or gelatinous consistency that becomes translucent when wet",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Microscopic Spores",
      "description": "Produces characteristic yeast-like spores that are best observed under microscopic examination",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Habitat Association",
      "description": "Found on decaying wood and plant debris, often appearing in clusters or aggregations",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Syzygospora mycetophila thrives in moist, shaded forest environments of New England, particularly on decomposing wood, leaf litter, and other organic substrates.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Despite its common name 'Collybia Clouds,' this fungus is actually a yeast-like basidiomycete that lacks the typical mushroom cap-and-stem structure, instead forming ephemeral gelatinous masses that appear and disappear with moisture levels.",
